WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.490 --> 00:00:02.500
Film Courage:&nbsp; Jen, are you a writer?

00:00:02.500 --> 00:00:03.850
Jen Grisanti:&nbsp; I am not.&nbsp;

00:00:03.850 --> 00:00:05.000
I am an author.&nbsp;

00:00:05.000 --> 00:00:11.690
It’s interesting because people ask me all
the time and it catches me off guard and I

00:00:11.690 --> 00:00:14.230
have to remember, yes, I’ve written three
books.&nbsp;

00:00:14.230 --> 00:00:18.869
I am an author so I am a writer and in that
sense, I am not a screenwriter.

00:00:18.869 --> 00:00:22.390
Film Courage:&nbsp; So what is it that you do
aside from the books?

00:00:22.390 --> 00:00:25.259
Jen Grisanti:&nbsp; I’m a speaker.&nbsp;

00:00:25.259 --> 00:00:28.000
I’m a blogger for the Huffington Post.&nbsp;

00:00:28.000 --> 00:00:32.130
I am a writing instructor for Writers on Verge
of NBC.&nbsp;

00:00:32.130 --> 00:00:37.860
I’ve been a writing instructor there for
eight years and I’m a story career consultant

00:00:37.860 --> 00:00:39.590
at Jen Grisanti consultancy.

00:00:39.590 --> 00:00:44.750
Film Courage:&nbsp; When someone approaches you
through your website and they say “I’m

00:00:44.750 --> 00:00:52.500
considering hiring you,” how can you determine
between a writing that is dabbling in the

00:00:52.500 --> 00:00:58.590
industry versus those who say this is my chosen
career path?

00:00:58.590 --> 00:01:02.030
Can tell the difference in terms of commitment
level?

00:01:02.030 --> 00:01:05.050
Jen Grisanti:&nbsp; You know, it’s interesting.&nbsp;

00:01:05.050 --> 00:01:13.409
I get writers who are coming out of college
to a co-executive producer, staff writer all

00:01:13.409 --> 00:01:15.520
the way up to co-executive producer level.&nbsp;

00:01:15.520 --> 00:01:23.390
This is because I was a former 12-year studio
executive and when I was an executive, I staffed

00:01:23.390 --> 00:01:26.659
over 15 top prime time TV shows.&nbsp;

00:01:26.659 --> 00:01:36.369
And I got to know a lot of writers, whereas
I know a lot of consultants get the brand,

00:01:36.369 --> 00:01:37.680
brand new writers.&nbsp;

00:01:37.680 --> 00:01:44.999
But because I have been exposed to the professional
writer I do get a lot of professional writers.&nbsp;

00:01:44.999 --> 00:01:53.429
I think my feeling, as part of being a current
programming executive, really was identifying

00:01:53.429 --> 00:01:55.420
new voices.&nbsp;

00:01:55.420 --> 00:02:07.819
I like working with the green writer who hasn’t
developed bad habits and who is open to the

00:02:07.819 --> 00:02:17.510
process, so I really don’t have a problem
if I have someone who is dabbling versus totally

00:02:17.510 --> 00:02:24.720
committed as long as they are committed to
the process that they signed on for.

00:02:24.720 --> 00:02:29.030
Film Courage:&nbsp; You said something that caught
my ear and that was bad habits.&nbsp;

00:02:29.030 --> 00:02:34.630
So in terms of being an established writer
or someone who has done it for several years

00:02:34.630 --> 00:02:39.730
and has “their way of doing things,” what
are some different tip-offs where you see

00:02:39.730 --> 00:02:44.990
this person is more all closed arms and not
open to the process?

00:02:44.990 --> 00:02:46.930
Jen Grisanti:&nbsp; There are many tip-offs.&nbsp;

00:02:46.930 --> 00:02:56.150
I would say probably the biggest tip-off is
the inability to hear a note before defending

00:02:56.150 --> 00:02:58.500
the note.&nbsp;

00:02:58.500 --> 00:03:03.670
I think the biggest tip-off …well, I mean
the first tip-off is on the page.&nbsp;

00:03:03.670 --> 00:03:14.210
You can tell in the first three pages if a
writer knows how to write.&nbsp;

00:03:14.210 --> 00:03:21.860
What I can also say is you can also tell a
voice and you can have a writer who has a

00:03:21.860 --> 00:03:27.170
voice but doesn’t have the formatting down.&nbsp;

00:03:27.170 --> 00:03:35.900
The voice can still be found through the bad
formatting, so that’s why I say I’m open

00:03:35.900 --> 00:03:39.260
and I have no problem working with brand new
writers.&nbsp;

00:03:39.260 --> 00:03:40.510
I love that.&nbsp;

00:03:40.510 --> 00:03:46.750
I also love working with produced writers
who do have a stronger sense of the craft.&nbsp;

00:03:46.750 --> 00:03:55.840
However, the gift is really in recognizing
when you have the newer writer who makes faux

00:03:55.840 --> 00:04:02.440
pas like defending a note before hearing a
note which is a very big mistake that many

00:04:02.440 --> 00:04:03.580
newer writers make.&nbsp;

00:04:03.580 --> 00:04:07.700
Also, it’s a mistake that writers make on
staff.&nbsp;

00:04:07.700 --> 00:04:18.039
And so it really is helping them to feel safe
when you’re giving a note.

00:04:18.039 --> 00:04:27.069
I can say when I was a studio executive, the
agenda was that of the network and the studio

00:04:27.069 --> 00:04:28.800
that I was carrying.&nbsp;

00:04:28.800 --> 00:04:37.580
Giving notes as a story career consultant
independently I have no agenda other than

00:04:37.580 --> 00:04:39.770
making the story the best that it could be.&nbsp;

00:04:39.770 --> 00:04:47.300
So this is why I encourage writers to be open
and to recognize my only goal is to make their

00:04:47.300 --> 00:04:49.000
story the best that it can be.

00:04:49.000 --> 00:04:55.360
Film Courage:&nbsp; You said something earlier
about you can overlook maybe formatting if

00:04:55.360 --> 00:04:57.919
the [writer’s] voice is strong and authentic.&nbsp;

00:04:57.919 --> 00:05:02.240
Does that mean if the story is original or
it has nothing to do with that?

00:05:02.240 --> 00:05:03.490
Jen Grisanti:&nbsp; No.&nbsp;

00:05:03.490 --> 00:05:04.490
It does.&nbsp;

00:05:04.490 --> 00:05:09.639
You know voice comes down to so many different
things.&nbsp;

00:05:09.639 --> 00:05:13.860
A voice could come down to character description.&nbsp;

00:05:13.860 --> 00:05:21.330
It could come down to writing poetic and lyrical
action lines.&nbsp;

00:05:21.330 --> 00:05:24.699
It could come down to the original concept.&nbsp;

00:05:24.699 --> 00:05:32.810
It could come down to knowing how to write
a flawed character well.&nbsp;

00:05:32.810 --> 00:05:39.259
I mean there are so many ways … I always
try to figure out if I could really pinpoint

00:05:39.259 --> 00:05:43.509
what voice is and help people understand.&nbsp;

00:05:43.509 --> 00:05:52.639
I think for me a large way that I define voice
is an idea of what is your world view?&nbsp;

00:05:52.639 --> 00:06:00.840
What has happened to you in your life that
has caused you pain and given you something

00:06:00.840 --> 00:06:06.780
to say and how does your writing reflect that?

00:06:06.780 --> 00:06:15.020
Film Courage:&nbsp; Have you ever seen someone
be too emotional and too…because we all

00:06:15.020 --> 00:06:19.499
feel that our story is intense and it is because
we’ve lived it.&nbsp;

00:06:19.499 --> 00:06:23.490
But have you ever seen it in the writing where
possibly a writer should tone it down, whatever

00:06:23.490 --> 00:06:25.229
it is, the anger in one scene or something?

00:06:25.229 --> 00:06:26.229
Jen Grisanti:&nbsp; Oh, yeah.&nbsp;

00:06:26.229 --> 00:06:33.740
I mean I would say a much great problem is
not enough emotion.&nbsp;

00:06:33.740 --> 00:06:38.689
I think too much emotion is not nearly as
common.&nbsp;

00:06:38.689 --> 00:06:45.689
It’s more the how do we elevate the emotion
so that we feel this more.

00:06:45.689 --> 00:06:52.940
Film Courage:&nbsp; And when you see this do you
feel like that writer is phoning in that character?&nbsp;

00:06:52.940 --> 00:06:56.740
It’s like filler or is it that maybe they’re
not totally in touch?

00:06:56.740 --> 00:07:01.150
Jen Grisanti:&nbsp; I think that comes from being
green and not really understanding.&nbsp;

00:07:01.150 --> 00:07:07.389
There’s so much that goes into strong character
work.&nbsp;

00:07:07.389 --> 00:07:16.040
I work with writers on the idea of thinking
about the wound.&nbsp;

00:07:16.040 --> 00:07:25.229
Thinking about the childhood wounds and in
a TV pilot, how this series of triggers splits

00:07:25.229 --> 00:07:27.099
open that wound.&nbsp;

00:07:27.099 --> 00:07:34.389
And then the idea of what is the negative
narrative that has come from that wound and

00:07:34.389 --> 00:07:39.749
how is that getting in the way of the central
character achieving the goal?

00:07:39.749 --> 00:07:47.719
And then how, through the pursuit, is the
central one step toward healing that wound?

00:07:47.719 --> 00:07:47.999
Question for the viewers:&nbsp; Are you quick
to defend your writing?

